# Agent Routing

Use this Rule when delegating SmartFlow WST/CPT package work or Jira batch ticket work to subagents, when routing `grid-cards` work to its write owners, and when orchestrating generic worker subagents (for example in `auto-docs`). The six agent definitions ship with this plugin; the three implementers/runners are thin runners around the bundled Skills.

## Packages

A **CPT package** is the delivery of one CPT across its surfaces: the foundation (registration, taxonomy, ACF group), cards, archive/grid, filters/carousels, optional single, and Sections embedding the grid. A **package run** covers more than one of these surfaces in one task. The **package manifest** is the main chat's decomposition record from Orchestration point 1 — per leaf task the goal and success criterion, affected area, dependencies, and the assigned write scope — and is where every spawn's write scope comes from. Package orchestration is not a separate mode and has no trigger of its own: every routing statement in this Rule applies on every start, single-surface direct starts included; a package run only adds more leaves to the same pattern.

## Roles And Delegation

| Agent | Role | Delegates into | Write scope |
|---|---|---|---|
| `cpt-codebase-analyst` | read-only inventory: reference baseline, layout profile, precedence, minimal file scope, evidenced route + baseline delta; runtime facts over the status bridge | — (readonly) | none |
| `cpt-figma-analyst` | package surface index mapped against the reference baseline (deviations only by default); full raw specs + PNG exports only for `pixel-parity` or complex components | — | package index, needed surface/component specs, explicitly required reference PNGs (docs layer) |
| `wst-shortcode-implementer` | thin runner: exactly one assigned foundation or section task | `wst-new-post-type` (once per package) or `wst-section-workflow` (one per flexible section) | project-owned WST templates/partials + ACF JSON field groups + the work record the Skill owns |
| `wpgb-specialist` | WPGB grid/card config from the baseline precedent (minimal diff), config math, runtime contract, route-back fixes | — | WPGB config apply-spec via the status bridge readback (or a project-validated write route); card CSS only with explicit file assignment in the manifest |
| `cpt-visual-implementer` | thin runner: exactly one assigned surface | `frontend-section-qa` (flexible WST sections) or `cpt-frontend-qa` (cards, grids, archives, fixed segments, singles) | assigned CSS/SCSS file group + its work-record section |
| `jira-ticket-runner` | thin runner: exactly one Jira subtask classified as direct-fix by the `jira-batch-workflow` orchestrator | `jira-ticket-workflow` (steps 3–5) | assigned file group inside the child theme (+ style-loader entry only if assigned); never commits, Jira writes, or WPGB config |

## Deliberately Not Routed

`content`, `wp-media-import`, `grill-me`, `smartflow-feedback`, `sync-project-brief`, `project-css-setup`, the setup and migration wizards (`setup-local-project`, `setup-acf-local-json`, `migrate-ssh-to-local`, `install-status-bridge`, `section-preview-harness`), and the pure reference Skills (`wst-shortcodes`, `smarttags`, `wst-nested-shortcodes`, `headline-filling`) deliberately run in the main chat: no bundled agent matches their work, they contain interactive hard stops, or they are lookup material rather than execution runs. `smartflow-feedback` needs the session context for autofill and the reporter for confirmations; `sync-project-brief` lives on the interactive diff review — and per the `confluence-source` Rule, runners never call Confluence themselves. `project-css-setup` lives on interactive per-value confirmations (theme vs design plus the affected-Sections impact list, decided by the user per value) and on hard stops between its resumable blocks — a runner would either block on every value or decide global CSS values alone. `section-preview-harness` additionally needs the `theme-functions.php` bootstrap confirmation, which the `wst-shortcode-implementer` runner is forbidden to give: when the preview-pages offer of `wst-section-workflow` surfaces inside a runner execution, it comes back as `OPEN DECISION` and the main chat runs the harness itself. This scope decision is intentional, not a gap.

## Model Routing

All six agent files ship with `model: inherit`, so a spawn never fails because a model is unavailable: by default every agent runs on the main chat's model. The table below lists the **recommended** model per agent, applied through dynamic pinning at spawn time:

| Agent | Recommended model |
|---|---|
| `cpt-codebase-analyst` | `composer-2.5-fast` (readonly) |
| `cpt-figma-analyst` | `gpt-5.6-sol-high` |
| `wst-shortcode-implementer` | `composer-2.5-fast` |
| `wpgb-specialist` | `claude-sonnet-5-thinking-high` |
| `cpt-visual-implementer` | `composer-2.5-fast` |
| `jira-ticket-runner` | `composer-2.5-fast` |

Recommended slugs age with the model lineup: when a slug in this table no longer exists on the account, the availability check below falls back to `inherit` — treat a permanently missing slug as a maintenance signal to update this table, not as a runtime problem. Before the first spawn of a run, the main chat checks whether the recommended slug is available on the account in use. Available: pass the slug at spawn. Not available: spawn with `inherit` and report the deviation transparently in one sentence — no blocker, no silent substitution by a third model. For the two demanding agents (`wpgb-specialist`, `cpt-figma-analyst`), if only a clearly weaker model is available, the main chat says so before the spawn. Premium escalation happens only after one evidenced gate conflict, decided by the main chat.

## Shared Rules

- Maximum two agent levels: main chat -> leaf agent. Leaf agents never spawn further subagents. A full context window is a `handoff` back to the main chat, not a nested spawn.
- Exactly one write owner per artifact or file group, assigned via the package manifest.
- Implementation semantics live in the bundled SmartFlow Skills; the runners load and follow them instead of restating their rules.
- Subagents never commit or push; the main chat commits and hands over per the `deploy-and-branches` Rule.
- No agent runs as a background agent by default. Exception: `jira-batch-workflow` launches its `jira-ticket-runner` leaves in the background by design so the main chat stays responsive for feedback relay.
- Every agent ends with the fixed return format (STATUS, EVIDENCE, OWN CHANGES, GATES, OPEN DECISION, NEXT OWNER) defined in its agent file. `STATUS` may be `handoff` when the leaf must stop because its context is filling.

The agents install with the plugin; the main chat invokes them by name (`/cpt-codebase-analyst` etc.) or lets Cursor route by description.

## Orchestration

The main chat is the orchestrator. It plans, spawns, stores handoffs, and integrates. It does not do the leaf's large file reads or long tool chains in its own context.

1. **Decompose with measure.** Split only when the work has more than one write owner or a real dependency chain. For each leaf task record: goal and success criterion, affected area, dependencies, and the assigned write scope — this record is the package manifest. Skip splitting when one leaf is enough. For the four runner-backed Skills (`wst-section-workflow`, `wst-new-post-type`, `frontend-section-qa`, `cpt-frontend-qa`) the runner is the standard execution vehicle even on a direct start with a single leaf: "one leaf is enough" then means one runner, not no runner. The two analysts spawn per the Skills' discovery steps, not per package size: `wst-new-post-type` spawns the `cpt-codebase-analyst` for the preflight evidence on every foundation or remodel start (skip only against a verified reference baseline and Project Layout Profile already recorded in the docs layer or `PROJECT-CONTEXT.md`) and the `cpt-figma-analyst` whenever the source brief carries a design reference; `wst-section-workflow` keeps routine single-Section discovery in the main chat and delegates only heavy Figma extraction (pixel-parity targets, genuinely complex components) to the `cpt-figma-analyst`.
2. **Self-contained prompts.** Every spawn includes the full assigned task, the relevant package context, worker constraints from this Rule and `deploy-and-branches`, and — on resume — the last handoff. The leaf does not see the parent chat or earlier user messages.
3. **Parallel only when independent.** Launch disjoint write artifacts in one message. Tasks that touch the same files or build on each other's results run sequential. When in doubt, sequential.
4. **Context-full continuation.** If a leaf's context is filling (long session, large file dumps, an upcoming context summary), it finishes the current step cleanly — no half edit, no command without a result — and returns `STATUS: handoff` plus the schema below. The main chat writes that return unchanged (short metadata header only) to `tmp/<task-slug>/handoff-<agent>-<nn>.md` and respawns the **same** agent type with that file. Leaves never continue themselves by spawning.
5. **Stall stop.** If a handoff shows no progress versus the previous one, or a single leaf task has more than 3 handoff resumes, stop and ask the user. Do not restart the same assignment endlessly.
6. **Secrets.** Never copy credential values into worker prompts or handoff files. Name the environment variables only (`secrets` Rule) and point at the repo-root `.env`.
7. **Integrate.** After every leaf reports `done` (or the visual-implementer equivalent), check that the parts fit: no overlapping file edits, no contradictory work-record fields. Then the main chat commits and hands over.

Handoff files stay in repo-level `tmp/` (outside the deploy path, not committed) until the user asks to delete them.

## Handoff Schema

A `handoff` must be fully self-contained — the next leaf knows neither the user message nor the parent transcript:

- **Task and goal** — the leaf assignment, its place in the package, and the success criterion ("done when …").
- **Current state** — what is already done, which files or records changed (concrete paths), committed vs uncommitted.
- **Tried — worked** — approaches, commands, and facts that helped.
- **Tried — failed** — failed approaches with the error or reason, so they are not repeated.
- **Facts** — paths, IDs, API behaviour, project quirks needed to continue.
- **Next steps** — remaining to-dos in order.
- **Constraints** — off-limits paths, no commit/push, deploy-and-branches, assigned write scope.
